#subsec("Affine Sets (Linear Manifolds)") // Tyrtyshnikov §13.1


Let $W$ be a subspace in a vector space $V$ and $bf(x)$ be some vector from $V$. A set of vectors of the form
$ M = {bf(v) = bf(x) + bf(w) | bf(w) in W}, $
that is, the translate of the subspace $W$ by the vector $bf(x)$, is called an _affine set_ (or _linear manifold_) in $V$. Notation: $M = bf(x) + W$.

The subspace $W$ is called the _direction subspace_ for $M$ and is uniquely determined by the set $M$.

Indeed, let
$ M = bf(x)_1 + W_1 = bf(x)_2 + W_2. $
From this, $bf(x)_1 - bf(x)_2 in W_1 sect W_2$. Let $bf(y) in W_1$. Then $bf(y) + (bf(x)_1 - bf(x)_2) in W_2 => bf(y) in W_2$. Similarly, if $bf(y) in W_2$, then $bf(y) + (bf(x)_2 - bf(x)_1) in W_1 => bf(y) in W_1$. #h(1fr) $square.stroked$

For $M = bf(x) + W$, the vector $bf(x)$ is called a _shift vector_. As the shift vector one can take any vector from $M$:
$ M = bf(x) + W = bf(y) + W quad forall bf(y) in M. $
Indeed, let $bf(y) = bf(x) + bf(w)_0$ for some $bf(w)_0 in W$. Then, if $bf(z) = bf(x) + bf(w)$ for some $bf(w) in W$, we have $bf(z) = bf(y) + (bf(x) - bf(y)) + bf(w) = bf(y) + (-bf(w)_0 + bf(w))$. Hence, $bf(x) + W subset.eq bf(y) + W$. The reverse inclusion is proved similarly. #h(1fr) $square.stroked$

If $W$ is a finite-dimensional space, then its dimension is also called the _dimension of the affine set_ $M = bf(x) + W$.

#example(title: "1")[
  The set of solutions of a linear system $bf(A) bf(x) = bf(b)$ with an $m times n$ matrix $bf(A)$ of rank $r$ is an affine set $bf(v) + W$, where $bf(v)$ is a particular solution of this system and $W = Null(bf(A))$ is the solution space of the associated homogeneous system $bf(A) bf(x) = bf(0)$.
]

#example(title: "2")[
  A line in the plane or in three-dimensional space is an affine set of dimension 1. A plane in three-dimensional space is an affine set of dimension 2.
]

#indent When studying affine sets, the elements of a vector space are usually called _points_. By analogy with geometric space, one can think of vectors as position vectors laid off from a common initial point identified with the zero vector.

