#sec("3.5", "Linear Independence")

#subsec("Linear Dependence") // Tyrtyshnikov §3.3


Vectors all of whose entries are equal to zero are called _zero vectors_, and sometimes simply _zeros_. We shall denote any zero vector by the symbol $bf(0)$.

A linear combination of vectors is called _nontrivial_ if at least one of its coefficients is nonzero. A system (in other words, a nonempty ordered collection of a finite number) of vectors is called _linearly dependent_ if there exists for them a nontrivial linear combination equal to the zero vector. (MA1522 speaks of a _set_ ${bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}$; a system lists its vectors in a fixed order and may contain repeated vectors.)

#lemma(title: "1")[
  If $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$ is a linearly dependent system of $k > 1$ nonzero vectors, then there exists in it a vector $bf(a)_m$, $m > 1$, that is a linear combination of the preceding vectors:
  $ bf(a)_m in span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_(m-1)}. $
]

#proof[
  Consider a nontrivial linear combination equal to zero:
  $ alpha_1 bf(a)_1 + ... + alpha_k bf(a)_k = bf(0), $
  and let $m$ be the largest index such that $alpha_m != 0$. If $m = 1$, then $alpha_1 bf(a)_1 = bf(0)$ and, since $alpha_1 != 0$, we obtain $bf(a)_1 = bf(0)$, which contradicts the hypothesis of the lemma. Consequently, $m > 1$. Then
  $ alpha_1 bf(a)_1 + ... + alpha_m bf(a)_m = bf(0) quad => quad bf(a)_m = (- alpha_1 / alpha_m) bf(a)_1 + ... + (- alpha_(m-1) / alpha_m) bf(a)_(m-1). $
]

#subsec("Linear Independence") // Tyrtyshnikov §3.4


A system of vectors is called _linearly independent_ if it is not linearly dependent. Thus, if the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$ are linearly independent, then
$ alpha_1 bf(a)_1 + ... + alpha_k bf(a)_k = bf(0) quad => quad alpha_1 = ... = alpha_k = 0. $

#lemma(title: "2")[
  Every subsystem of a linearly independent system is linearly independent.
]

#proof[
  Suppose that a subsystem is linearly dependent. This means that there exists a nontrivial linear combination of the vectors of this subsystem equal to zero. Then the linear combination of the vectors of the original system with the same coefficients for the vectors from the subsystem and zero coefficients for the other vectors is a nontrivial linear combination equal to zero. We obtain a contradiction with the linear independence of the original system.
]

#lemma(title: "3")[
  If a vector is a linear combination of linearly independent vectors, then the coefficients of this linear combination are uniquely determined.
]

#proof[
  Suppose the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$ are linearly independent and
  $ bf(b) = alpha_1 bf(a)_1 + ... + alpha_k bf(a)_k = beta_1 bf(a)_1 + ... + beta_k bf(a)_k. $
  Hence
  $ (alpha_1 - beta_1) bf(a)_1 + ... + (alpha_k - beta_k) bf(a)_k = bf(0) quad => quad alpha_1 - beta_1 = ... = alpha_k - beta_k = 0. $
]

